    {
      "id": "rules",
      "duration": 4.5,
      "narration": "So how do fast apps stay fast? They follow a handful of simple rules.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "icon",
          "name": "zap",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 38,
          "size": 22,
          "draw": 0.8,
          "color": "#1e6fd9",
          "start": 0.3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "How fast apps stay fast",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 72,
          "size": 9,
          "draw": 1.0,
          "start": 2.7
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fixes",
      "duration": 14.1,
      "narration": "Cache what you read often, so the data lives closer. Use a C D N, so your servers live closer to your users. Batch work into fewer round trips. Do independent work in parallel. And measure the slowest requests, not the average.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "x": 18,
          "y": 18,
          "size": 6,
          "spacing": 1.75,
          "draw": 13,
          "items": [
            "Cache: keep data close",
            "CDN: keep servers close to users",
            "Batch: fewer round trips",
            "Parallel: don't wait in line",
            "Measure p99, not the average"
          ],
          "start": 0.3
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "close",
      "duration": 7.9,
      "narration": "Fast software is less about faster code, and more about fewer, shorter trips. Count your round trips, and you will find your missing second.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Count your round trips",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 36,
          "size": 10,
          "draw": 1.4,
          "start": 0.3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "latency = distance x number of trips",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 60,
          "size": 6,
          "draw": 1.4,
          "color": "#1e6fd9",
          "start": 3.3
        },
        {
          "type": "icon",
          "name": "timer",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 84,
